    "description": "If you want to start the generator from indoors during a storm, this is the one. Westinghouse lists electric, remote and recoil start, and rates the iGen5000DF at 3,900 running and 5,000 peak watts on gasoline and 3,500 running and 4,500 peak on propane. Its 22-hour quarter-load run time on a 20 lb cylinder is the longest listed among the mid-size picks, and the 3.4-gallon gas tank is rated for 18 hours at the same load. The noise rating is 52 dBA, the lowest of the mid-size units, though Westinghouse does not say at what distance it was measured. The panel has a 5-20R duplex, a TT-30R for RVs and two USB ports, and it is parallel capable. The catch is safety: this version has no CO sensor. The iGen5000DFcv adds one but is recoil start only. Westinghouse's site now shows this model on the page that used to carry the iGen4500DF, which its older spec sheet rated at 3,700 / 3,330 running watts, so check which version a listing is selling."
  },
  {
    "id": "champion-201417",
    "name": "Champion 11,000-Watt Electric Start Dual Fuel Inverter Generator with CO Shield (201417)",
    "category": "9,000W running gas / 8,100W propane, 120/240V",
    "badge": "Best for Home Hookup",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "specs": [
