        "64 dBA at 23 ft"
      ],
      [
        "Weight",
        "205.1 lb"
      ],
      [
        "Outlets",
        "2x 5-20R GFCI, L14-30R 30A, 14-50R (37.5A), 12V DC"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "120/240V with L14-30R and 14-50R outlets for an inlet and transfer switch",
      "11,000 starting watts on both fuels for pumps and compressors",
      "CO Shield shutoff",
      "Electric start, 459cc engine",
      "3-year limited warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No parallel capability",
      "205.1 lb, so it lives on its wheels",
      "8 hours per 20 lb cylinder at a quarter load"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The dual fuel inverter for running house circuits through a transfer switch, including a 240V well pump.",
    "description": "This is the dual fuel inverter for people who want to run house circuits rather than extension cords. Champion rates the 201417 at 9,000 running watts on gasoline and 8,100 on propane, with 11,000 starting watts on both fuels, enough to start a 240V well pump or a central air compressor that a 120V inverter cannot touch. The panel has two GFCI 5-20R duplexes, a 30-amp L14-30R and a 14-50R that Champion limits to 37.5 amps, so an electrician can connect it through an inlet box to a transfer switch or interlock. CO Shield is standard, and the 64 dBA rating is measured at 23 feet. The trade-offs are size and fuel. At 205.1 lb it lives on its wheels, and Champion lists 8 hours on a 20 lb cylinder at a quarter load, so a long outage on propane needs a 100 lb tank or a stack of cylinders. It does not support parallel operation."
  },
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-igen12000tfc",
    "name": "Westinghouse iGen12000TFc Tri-Fuel Inverter Generator with CO Sensor",
    "category": "9,000W gas / 8,100W propane / 7,200W natural gas running",
    "badge": "Best Tri Fuel",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Running Watts (Gas / LP / NG)",
        "9,000 / 8,100 / 7,200"
      ],
      [
        "Peak Watts (Gas / LP / NG)",
        "12,000 / 11,000 / 9,800"
      ],
      [
        "Propane Run Time (20 lb)",
        "9 hr at 25% / 5 hr at 50% load"
      ],
      [
