    "cons": [
      "Natural gas output is 20 percent below gasoline",
      "Needs a properly installed gas connection",
      "Noise rated only as low as 64 dBA, distance not stated"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best choice if your house has natural gas service and you want a portable that never needs refueling.",
    "description": "If your house has a natural gas meter, this tri fuel inverter can run from the utility line for as long as the gas keeps flowing. Westinghouse rates it at 9,000 running watts on gasoline, 8,100 on propane and 7,200 on natural gas, with peaks of 12,000, 11,000 and 9,800. It is 120/240V with an L14-30R, a 14-50R and a GFCI 5-20R duplex, and Westinghouse lists it as transfer switch ready. A CO sensor is standard, and start is electric, remote or recoil. On propane it is thirsty: 9 hours at a quarter load and 5 hours at half load on a 20 lb cylinder, against 19 and 11 hours on the 7.9-gallon gas tank. Noise is rated as low as 64 dBA, and dry weight is 191 lb. Naming is in flux: Westinghouse's iGen11000TFc page now displays this model as the iGen12000TFc, with the same running ratings and higher peaks, and retailers still list both. If you have no gas line, the dual fuel iGen12000DFc carries the same gasoline and propane ratings."
  },
  {
    "id": "champion-201183",
    "name": "Champion 2500-Watt Dual Fuel Inverter Generator with CO Shield (201183)",
    "category": "1,850W running gas / 1,665W propane, 39 lb",
    "badge": "Best Lightweight",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "specs": [
      [
        "Running Watts (Gas / Propane)",
        "1,850 / 1,665"
      ],
      [
