        "2,500 / 2,500"
      ],
      [
        "Run Time",
        "Up to 11.5 hr gas (1.1 gal) / up to 34 hr propane (20 lb)"
      ],
      [
        "Rated Noise",
        "53 dBA"
      ],
      [
        "Weight",
        "39 lb"
      ],
      [
        "Outlets",
        "120V 5-20R, 12V DC, parallel"
      ]
    ],
    "pros": [
      "39 lb, the lightest generator on this list",
      "CO Shield shutoff",
      "Up to 34 hours on a 20 lb cylinder",
      "Parallel ready for a second unit later",
      "3-year warranty"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "1,665 propane watts covers a fridge and small loads, not an RV air conditioner",
      "Recoil start only, no 30-amp RV outlet",
      "Champion's sell sheet does not state the load behind its run times"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The dual fuel inverter to carry to a campsite or keep for a fridge-and-lights outage plan.",
    "description": "The 201183 is the one to carry to a campsite or keep for a fridge-and-lights outage plan. Champion rates it at 1,850 running watts on gasoline and 1,665 on propane, with 2,500 starting watts on either fuel, from a 79cc engine. At 39 lb it is the lightest generator on this list by more than 10 pounds, and its 53 dBA rating is among the lowest. Champion lists up to 11.5 hours on the 1.1-gallon gas tank and up to 34 hours on a 20 lb propane cylinder, though the sell sheet does not state the load for those figures, so read them as light-load best cases. CO Shield is standard and it is parallel ready, so a second unit can be added later. Start is recoil only, and the panel has 120V 5-20R household outlets and a 12V DC outlet but no 30-amp RV outlet. Champion's site has moved some product pages, so confirm the model number on the listing you buy from."
