                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.53 gal</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read the load column before comparing. A quarter load on the iGen5000DF is under 900 watts on propane, roughly a refrigerator cycling plus lights and a modem; a half load on the iGen12000TFc is over 4,000 watts. The WEN's 14 hours and the Generac's 11 hours at half load are not a fair fight either, since half of the Generac's propane rating is 1,750 watts and half of the WEN's is 1,300.
          </p>

          <p>
            A full 20 lb cylinder holds about 4.7 gallons of propane. For a multi-day outage, plan on three or four filled cylinders, or a 100 lb tank with a longer hose. Propane keeps for years and does not gum up a carburetor the way stale gasoline does, which is the main reason to buy dual fuel.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most outages, start on the gasoline already in the tank, so it gets used before it ages, then switch to propane once the outage looks long.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inverter-vs-open-frame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Dual Fuel Inverter or Open-Frame Dual Fuel Generator?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy a dual fuel inverter if you want quiet, clean power and long propane run times at moderate loads. Buy an open-frame dual fuel generator if you need the most watts for the money or 240V output on a budget. The two overlap only at the top of this list.
          </p>

          <p>
            An inverter electronically rebuilds its output as a clean sine wave, which lets the engine change speed with the load. That is where the low noise ratings and long light-load run times come from. An open-frame generator spins at a fixed speed whatever the load, so it is louder and thirstier when lightly loaded.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Voltage decides the hookup</h3>

          <p>
            The five smaller inverters here are 120V only: three have a TT-30R RV outlet and the Generac has an L5-30R locking outlet. They run appliances through heavy-duty extension cords. The Champion 201417 and Westinghouse iGen12000TFc are 120/240V with L14-30R and 14-50R outlets, so an electrician can connect them through an inlet box and a transfer switch or interlock. Our <a href="/best-generator-transfer-switch/">generator transfer switch guide</a> covers matching the hardware.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are leaning toward a big unit, compare it with the open-frame options in our <a href="/best-dual-fuel-generator/">best dual fuel generator</a> guide first; they offer similar propane output but are louder. The inverter earns its place near a bedroom window, a neighbor's fence or a campground. For standby, portable and battery options side by side, start with our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator for power outages</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parallel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Is Running Two Dual Fuel Inverters in Parallel Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sometimes. Two mid-size dual fuel inverters linked with a parallel kit deliver close to double the output of one, and each can run from its own cylinder. All four brands list parallel capability on the 120V models here, each with its own kit sold separately.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case for it: two units rated at 3,500 propane watts give about 7,000, and on a light day you run just one, which is quieter and burns less fuel. Each half weighs about 100 lb; the 240V units here weigh 191 and 205 lb.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case against: parallel output is still 120V, so it will not run a 240V well pump or feed a 240V transfer switch, and you maintain two of everything. The Champion 201417 does not support parallel operation, and the iGen12000TFc pairs only with other 240V Westinghouse inverters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="co-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How Do You Run a Dual Fuel Inverter Safely?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Outdoors, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents. That is the U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission's guidance, and it applies to propane exactly as it does to gasoline. Propane burns cleaner, but a propane-fueled engine still produces carbon monoxide, and CPSC estimates that portable generators cause nearly 80 carbon monoxide deaths a year on average.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">CARBON MONOXIDE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Quiet is not the same as safe.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A quiet inverter tempts people to run it in a garage, on a porch or under a carport. Do not. CO Shield, COsense and CO Watchdog shut the engine off if carbon monoxide builds up around the generator, but they do not protect the people inside. Put battery-backed CO alarms on every level of the house and outside sleeping areas.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Six of the seven picks have automatic CO shutoff. The exception is the Westinghouse iGen5000DF; the iGen5000DFcv adds a CO sensor but drops electric and remote start for recoil only.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane-specific checks</h3>

          <p>
            Use the hose and regulator that came with the generator, and check it for cracks before each season. Keep the cylinder upright, outside and away from the exhaust, close the valve when the generator is off, and never store cylinders in a basement or attached garage. If you smell gas at the connection, shut the valve and fix the fitting before restarting.
          </p>

          <p>
            In bad weather, use a generator tent or cover made for open-air operation rather than pulling the unit under an overhang, and buy a heavy-gauge cord long enough to reach 20 feet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes With Dual Fuel Inverter Generators
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing on the gasoline number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every generator here loses about 10 percent of its running watts on propane. A load list that fits 3,900 gasoline watts can trip the breaker at 3,500 on propane, especially when a refrigerator compressor and a sump pump start together. Build the list against the propane rating and leave 10 to 20 percent of margin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a 120V inverter to run the well pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Champion 201319, Generac iQ5200DF, Westinghouse iGen5000DF, Champion 201183 and WEN DF360iX are 120V only. Most submersible well pumps are 240V, and so are central air and electric dryers. For 240V circuits, buy the Champion 201417 or Westinghouse iGen12000TFc and have an electrician install an inlet and transfer switch.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing run times at different loads</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Makers quote propane run time at a quarter load, a half load or an unstated load, and a quarter load on a 3,500-watt inverter is under 900 watts. Check which load each figure assumes, and plan cylinders for the load you will actually run.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the base model when you wanted CO shutoff</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some dual fuel inverters are sold with and without a CO sensor under nearly identical names, such as the Westinghouse iGen5000DF and iGen5000DFcv. Read the full model number. CO shutoff is cheap insurance, never a substitute for the 20-foot rule and indoor CO alarms.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a small cylinder flat out on a cold night</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Propane pressure drops as a 20 lb cylinder gets cold, and output can sag below the rating just when you need the furnace blower most. Keep the tank out of the wind, avoid running at the top of the propane rating in freezing weather, and consider a larger cylinder for winter outages.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

