        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best dual fuel inverter generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes and RVs, the Champion 4500-Watt Electric Start Dual Fuel Inverter (201319): 3,150 running watts on propane, 61 dBA at 23 feet, CO Shield and a TT-30R outlet at 99.2 lb. If you need 240V for a transfer switch, the Champion 201417 and the Westinghouse iGen12000TFc are both rated at 8,100 running watts on propane.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much power does an inverter generator lose on propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About 10 percent of running watts for every model on this list. The Champion 201319 goes from 3,500 to 3,150 running watts, the Generac iQ5200DF and Westinghouse iGen5000DF from 3,900 to 3,500, and the 240V units from 9,000 to 8,100. Champion keeps starting watts equal on both fuels; Generac lowers them on propane too.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a dual fuel inverter run on a 20 lb propane tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on size and load. At a quarter load, Westinghouse lists 22 hours for the iGen5000DF, Champion 21 hours for the 201319 and Generac 20 hours for the iQ5200DF. The big 240V units manage 8 to 9 hours at a quarter load. At half load, Generac lists 11 hours for the iQ5200DF and Westinghouse 5 hours for the iGen12000TFc.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Honda make a dual fuel inverter generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not as a factory model in the U.S. Honda's inverter generators, including the EU2200i and EU3200i, run on gasoline. Propane-capable Hondas for sale are aftermarket conversions or kits, which can affect warranty coverage. For factory propane with a full maker's warranty, choose a brand on this list.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a dual fuel inverter generator power a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 120V model can power essentials through extension cords: a refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower, lights and internet fit under 3,500 propane watts if the motors start one at a time. Running household circuits through the panel needs a 120/240V unit like the Champion 201417 or Westinghouse iGen12000TFc, plus an electrician-installed inlet and transfer switch or interlock.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a dual fuel inverter run an RV air conditioner on propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, in the 3,500-watt class. A 13,500 BTU rooftop unit typically needs around 3,000 watts to start without a soft starter, which the Champion 201319, Generac iQ5200DF and Westinghouse iGen5000DF cover on propane. The 2,600-watt WEN DF360iX is a soft-starter case, and the 1,665-watt Champion 201183 is for RVs with no air conditioning load.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a tri fuel inverter generator worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if your house has natural gas service and you will pay a plumber to install a proper connection. The Westinghouse iGen12000TFc then runs at up to 7,200 running watts on natural gas with no refueling. Without a gas line the third fuel adds nothing; the dual fuel Champion 201417 does the same job.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
